body+=section('Go beyond the series number',chips([('Shoulder-screw size tables','Reference-MS51576.html'),('Slotted shoulder-screw tables','Reference-MS51575.html'),('Insert thread relationships','Reference-MS51830.html'),('Document status and successors','Resources-Mil-Spec-Cross-Reference.html'),('Technical table directory','Resources-Mil-Spec-Tables.html'),('Purchasing terminology','Resources-Mil-Spec-Terminology.html')]),'TECHNICAL REFERENCES','ref-tint')
body+=section('Reference information with a clear source','<p>Document status rows link to DLA records. Dimensional summaries identify the catalog or drawing revision used. The <a href="Resources-Mil-Spec-Sources.html">source register</a> distinguishes an official record from a manufacturer catalog and a historical reference.</p><p>Send Webson the full requirement for price and availability. This reference center does not represent a live inventory or a list of approved substitutions.</p>','USING THE REFERENCE CENTER')
page('Resources-Mil-Spec-Reference.html','Mil-Spec Fastener Reference | AN, MS, NAS & NASM | Webson','Search military and aerospace fastener series, hardware families, shoulder screw sizes, insert thread tables and sourced specification cross-references.','Mil-Spec fastener reference.',intro,body)
# Family content, written for the identification task rather than search-term variants.
F={
'Machine screws':('Identify the head, recess and thread.','A pan head sits above the bearing surface. A countersunk head fits a matching seat; the included angle is part of the requirement. A drilled fillister head adds another configuration that must be preserved.','Keep coarse and fine thread references distinct. MS51957 and MS51958 are separate series; MS24693 uses a 100-degree head while MS51959 is associated with an 82-degree head.','Head style and angle; drive/recess; nominal diameter; pitch; length convention; material and finish.'),
'Socket screws':('Separate drive type from the rest of the specification.','An internal hex drive does not by itself identify a socket screw. Material, thread class, head geometry and locking features remain part of the complete designation.','MS16995 and MS16996 identify corrosion-resistant socket screw families with different thread series. MS16997 is an alloy-steel family with a documented successor NAS1352.','Diameter and pitch; UNC/UNF; thread class; head clearance; locking feature; finish.'),
'Shoulder screws':('Measure the shoulder, not just the thread.','The smooth shoulder provides a locating or bearing surface. Its diameter and length are separate from the threaded end, so a thread-size match alone does not define the same component.','MS51575 is a slotted family; MS51576 uses a hex socket. Matching dash numbers do not always select matching shoulder lengths across these two series.','Shoulder diameter and length; drive; male thread; head clearance; bearing surface and tolerances.'),
'Hex cap screws':('Keep strength, thread and drilling requirements together.','The hex head is only one part of the requirement. Stainless, nickel-copper and carbon/alloy-steel cap screws are different families, with additional finish and drilled-feature requirements.','MS35307 and MS35308 are related corrosion-resistant families distinguished by thread series. MS90725 and MS90726 are cataloged Grade 5 families; they are not generic substitutes for stainless cap screws.','Grade/material; diameter and pitch; thread class; length; head/shank drilling; coating and test requirements.'),
'Washers':('Identify the washer function before comparing size.','Flat washers, tooth lock washers and helical split lock washers perform different roles. A common nominal screw size does not make these types equivalent.','AN960 and NAS1149 have a documented standard relationship. MS35333 and MS35335 are different tooth-lockwasher families; neither is simply a renamed flat washer.','Inside diameter; outside diameter; thickness; flat/locking type; material; finish; complete designation.'),
'Nuts':('Check the locking feature and envelope.','Plain, slotted, prevailing-torque and nonmetallic-insert nuts differ in more than appearance. Height, wrench size and bearing features can matter in an existing assembly.','MS21042 is a reduced-height locking family. A regular-height or insert-style locknut should be reviewed against the drawing rather than selected by matching thread size alone.','Thread and class; locking style; height and wrench clearance; material; finish; specified temperature/performance.'),
'Pins':('Choose by the retention and fit requirement.','Dowel, spring, cotter, clevis and quick-release pins serve different locating or retention tasks. Start with the pin type called out on the drawing.','MS16555 and MS16556 distinguish different over-nominal dowel families. A slotted tubular spring pin such as MS16562 is a different product, not an oversized solid dowel.','Pin style; diameter and tolerance; grip/length; end features; holes; material and protective treatment.'),
'Rivets':('Preserve head style, material and installation requirements.','Solid rivets and blind rivets use different installation methods. A flush head also requires a compatible seat; head angle and head dimensions are part of the selected family.','MS20426 is a countersunk precision-head family and MS20470 is a universal-head family. Do not select between them by diameter and length alone.','Solid/blind type; head geometry; material code; diameter; length or grip; finish; installation process.'),
'Threaded inserts':('Match both internal and external requirements.','The screw that enters an insert and the hole that receives it are two separate interfaces. Wall thickness, locking method and installation tooling must be considered with both threads.','MS51830, MS51831 and MS51832 identify lightweight, heavy-duty and extra-heavy-duty keylocking families. The internal thread size alone does not define the same outside envelope.','Internal/external thread; wall/duty series; length; key arrangement; internal locking; material; installation tooling.'),
'Set screws':('The point shape is part of the part.','Cup, flat and half-dog points interact differently with the mating component. Headless appearance or a common hex socket is not enough to establish an alternate.','The directory distinguishes cup-point MS51021/MS51023 from flat-point MS51031 and half-dog-point MS51047. Check the complete callout before considering a different point.','Thread and pitch; point geometry; length; material; finish; locking feature; intended mating surface.'),
'Retaining rings':('The shaft or bore groove controls the fit.','Internal and external rings fit different grooves. Ring section, thickness and installation style must agree with the design, not only the nominal shaft or bore diameter.','The MS16624-MS16634 group contains multiple ring configurations. The family numbers are an index, not a statement that every ring in that group can replace another.','Internal/external location; groove geometry; ring thickness; material; finish; installation access and tooling.'),
'Spacers & standoffs':('Start with the mounting interfaces.','A spacer has a through opening; a standoff uses threaded or installation interfaces to establish separation between parts. Round/hex shape alone does not identify the mounting arrangement.','NAS1829, NAS1830 and NAS1831 are cataloged as male-female, swage and female-female standoff families. Those interface differences matter when reviewing an existing assembly.','Body length; across-flats/outside diameter; bore or threads; male/female/swage interfaces; material; finish.'),
'Safety wire':('Keep wire requirements separate from fastener requirements.','Safety or lock wire is a separate item used with an assembly-specific installation method. The specified alloy, diameter and condition should accompany the wire designation.','A drilled fastener provides a feature for an intended retention method. Its presence does not establish the wire material, routing or installation requirement.','Full wire designation; diameter; alloy; condition; packaging; applicable installation/process instruction.')}
for family in families:
 a,b,c,checks=F[family];items=[r for r in series if r['family']==family]
 body=section(a,'<p>'+b+'</p><p>'+c+'</p>','IDENTIFICATION')
